From 267bb744b199aa0604d6290dffb02ccc563fc4b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: T <t@d.local>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 01:30:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] D11: force UTF-8 on stdout and stderr before the first print.
 When stdout is a PIPE, Python falls back to the locale encoding - cp1252 on
 the operator's machine - and the opening banner U+2550 has no cp1252 mapping,
 so the run died before any work. The earlier cp1252 patch fixed the arrow on
 the direct-console path and the piped path was never exercised.
 sys.stdout/stderr.reconfigure(encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') now runs as
 the first statement in main(), needing no PYTHONIOENCODING from the caller,
 and errors='replace' means an unmappable character degrades to a placeholder
 instead of killing a multi-hour scan at print time. Audited all eleven
 non-ASCII characters the pipeline can emit; six of them are unmappable in
 cp1252. Also gave every to_csv in master.py an explicit encoding='utf-8' so
 artifact writes cannot inherit a locale default.

---
 dot_master_discovery/master.py | 20 ++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

